To give a bit of a preview to the call and to allow some time to think before it, I have had some discussions with Gramercy following a meeting on Monday.
&nbsp;


As you may be aware (or we should make the insurers aware), there are various settlement discussions now advancing on NOx: 1. Nissan 2.Renault 3. Mercedes 4. Suzuki 5. Ford
In relation to BHP, we are hopeful of receiving judgment before the summer break starts (i.e. end of July) and in the(hopefully) very likely event we are successful, we will be seeking an interim payment on account of costs in
 a sum that will be 60% of 250m pounds+. Realistically, I expect an interim between 60-100m pounds.
PG has agreed terms for additional financing from Gramercy of circa $100m ($75m on BHP and $25m on NOx) to provide us with runway through to end of Q1 2027
In the event of two or more of the NOx cases and/or one of NOx and BHP positive liability judgment coming in, Gramercy are hoping and PG is looking to refinance Gramercy out (either all four facilities or
 the BHP ones) at a cheaper rate.
&nbsp;
I am cognisant of the fact that CPI policies, associated with our Gramercy facilities, are portable in a refi (with the consent of the insurers) and that there is an overlap of insurers between the CPI/ATE
 layers.
&nbsp;
The “issue” therefore that arises in terms of PG seeking to free up the interim payment on BHP, to use for opex, rather than hold on trust against any future adverse costs payments may be resolved by virtue
 of a simultaneous refinance of PG’s entire debt stack (or the BHP part) of the Brazilian lenders and Gramercy, transferring the CPI policies to a new (partially at least) insurance wrapped facility, either prepaying the D&amp;C premia or restructuring the policies
 and/or purchasing JPI against the judgment being overturned on appeal.
&nbsp;
You will no doubt have some more sophisticated thoughts than mine on this but I wanted to set this out for the purposes of discussion and also to see how Willis might be able to support a refi process.
&nbsp;
Thanks
Tom
