udgment).I’m not suggesting a further SFC application in July, my thinking is that one would never be required.&nbsp;My understanding is as follows:A. Claimants Win PDD (wholly or partially)No (or limited) adverse costs order for PDD in Defendants'&nbsp;favour → some or all existing ATE cover remains unused&nbsp;for that purpose.Quantum trial proceeds in October, but the PDD-era ATE resource can be “recycled”&nbsp;to cover the Defendants future adverse costs exposure (e.g. Quantum PTR, Trial Prep, Trial).Net result:&nbsp;No need for additional&nbsp;ATE or LOI cover for post July 2026 if the existing cover (from PDD) exceeds or matches quantum-phase risk (which does).B. Claimants Lose PDDAdverse costs order for PDD&nbsp;→ Current ATE is needed to satisfy that liability;&nbsp;therefore Claimants need sufficient ATE to cover costs up to July 2026.Quantum trial will not happen, since it follows logically from PDD success.Therefore: Quantum PTR, Trial Prep, and Trial phases will never be incurred -&nbsp;and there is no risk of further adverse costs&nbsp;after July 2026.Net result:&nbsp;No additional ATE or LOI needed beyond July 2026 (and any already-obtained for post-July 2026&nbsp;may be wasted, i.e. irrecoverable premium expense).Either way, the conclusion seems to be that no further ATE or LOI cover is required for the Defendants’ adverse costs post–July 2026 on any outcome.Happy to jump on a call and be corrected if I’m missing the point somewhere.Kind regards,Sarah​​​​Sarah&nbsp;BinghamLegal&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7484&nbsp;085&nbsp;187This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&